# What a change bulkhead-git-check reports can do, so you can judge it.
# Each line is a shell pattern, a level, then what a match means. The
# pattern matches "KIND:WHAT", e.g., "config:filter.lfs.smudge" or
# "hook:pre-commit"; the first match wins. git prints a key's section
# and name in lower case, so write them that way here. See
# compartments.md, "Git runs commands from local files".
#
# The level says whether adding a match needs your approval. "ask"
# means it can (1) run a program without your spelling it out, or (2)
# change where a plain git push goes (or let someone read it on the
# way); else it's "note", shown but recorded without asking. Something
# you have to spell out (e.g., "git push NAME") you'll notice anyway.
repo:* note a repository you hadn't seen: its risky settings, if any, are marked below.
error:* ask git couldn't read this, so the check can't see what's there; look at it by hand.
git-dir:* note where git keeps this repository: a change can mean a planted .git file pointing elsewhere (its settings are listed here).
common-dir:* note where git reads this repository's config and hooks: a planted .git/commondir redirects them (they're listed here).
push-to:* ask where a plain git push sends a branch: check you meant to push there.
hook:* ask a hook: git-hook-passthrough runs it only once you approve its hash, but check it anyway.
config:filter.* ask a filter driver: runs a command when git checks out or adds a file that .gitattributes assigns to it.
config:diff.external ask runs a command on every git diff.
config:diff.*.command ask a diff driver: runs a command when git diffs a file that .gitattributes assigns to it.
config:diff.*.textconv ask a diff driver: runs a command when git diffs a file that .gitattributes assigns to it.
config:merge.*.driver ask a merge driver: runs a command when git merges a file that .gitattributes assigns to it.
config:remote.*.uploadpack ask runs a command on fetch from a local or ssh remote.
config:remote.*.receivepack ask runs a command on push to a local or ssh remote.
config:remote.*.vcs ask runs the remote helper git-remote-VCS.
config:remote.*.proxy ask a proxy for this remote: it sees your push, and with certificate checks off can read it.
config:http.*proxy* ask an HTTP proxy: it sees your push, and with certificate checks off can read it.
config:http.*ssl* ask HTTPS certificate settings: turning off or changing checks lets someone read or redirect your push.
config:credential.* ask credential helpers: runs each one on push and gives it your password.
config:core.fsmonitor ask runs a command on most git commands (your login environment pins it, but cron and the like don't get that).
config:core.hookspath ask picks where hooks come from (your login environment pins it).
config:core.sshcommand ask runs a command to reach ssh remotes.
config:core.gitproxy ask runs a command to reach git:// remotes.
config:core.pager ask runs a command to show git's output.
config:core.editor ask runs a command when git wants a message from you.
config:core.askpass ask runs a command to ask for your password.
config:core.alternaterefscommand ask runs a command when git reads an alternate's refs.
config:sequence.editor ask runs a command during git rebase -i.
config:pager.* ask runs a command to show some git command's output.
config:alias.* ask an alias: overrides yours of that name, and one starting with "!" runs a command.
config:gpg.* ask picks the program git runs to sign or verify.
config:submodule.*.update ask a submodule's update: one starting with "!" runs a command on git submodule update (or a plain pull or checkout, with submodule.recurse).
config:submodule.* note a submodule's settings, e.g., where it fetches.
config:lfs.* ask Git LFS settings: e.g., lfs.url sends your LFS pushes elsewhere, and custom transfer agents run commands.
config:url.* note rewrites URLs (where a plain push goes is checked separately).
config:remote.* note a remote's settings: check it in git-config(1).
config:core.* note a core setting: check it in git-config(1).
config:* note not one I know to be risky; check it in git-config(1).
